Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Annealing Oven Spits Frost
- Story & Context: When private security teams cut the main power grid to Vault-11 outside Curvelo, night technician Beto watched twenty racks of heirloom black-bean seeds begin to crystallize under freezing vents. Desperate to generate friction and keep the squatted archive warm, he wired four contact mics directly to a pair of ceramic-shelled bongos and beat a frenzied 136-BPM tamborzão rhythm through the facility's emergency sirens. The resulting shockwave kept the shivering squad dancing for nine straight hours until the grid sputtered back to life, marking the violent birth of the vault-party network. Beto's unhinged opening roar became the scene’s founding battle cry: "Break the glass seal before the frost eats the kernel!"
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Macro close-up of a high-temperature industrial kiln window coated in thick frost, glowing red-hot from the inside while sharp blue ice crystals crack and shatter across the glass pane, lyric words glowing in melted soot.

Track 2: Sister Inês Behind the Stained Quartz
- Story & Context: Recorded during a forty-eight-hour military curfew inside an abandoned quartz-cutting warehouse in Diamantina, Sister Inês confessed her sabotage of the municipal pesticide depot into a mic covered in wet cotton. Her voice trembles with exhaustion over a stripped-back pattern of bare-knuckle bongo slaps and the low, suffocating 60-cycle electrical hum of dormant diamond drills. Sweat dripped directly onto the mixing desk as she documented the exact chemical codes she replaced with saline, knowing her arrest warrant was already circulating through the province. She whispers the chorus through broken teeth between breathless percussion rolls: "You can scrub the stained quartz clean, but the runoff remembers my name."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Atmospheric chiaroscuro portrait of a woman in work clothes sitting beside a massive mosaic window made of rough-cut stained quartz slabs, dust motes floating in sharp beams of deep amber and violet sunlight, bongo drums resting on the stone floor beside her.

Track 3: Quem Soprou Esse Fogo?
- Story & Context: When federal agents finally stormed the Serra do Espinhaço vault complex, they discovered empty barracks littered with broken bongo shells, trampled flour, and cooling fans locked into an endless mechanical feedback loop. The squad found no occupants, but the sound system had been welded into the structural beams, continuously firing a thunderous, syncopated 140-BPM riot-funk pulse down the ventilation shafts into the valley below. Local farmers gathered outside the perimeter fences, cheering as the sub-bass rattled the armed personnel carriers and shook pollen from the trees like golden rain. Scrawled across the blast doors in molten silica was the unsolved taunt that gave the track its legend: "Who blew this fire if the furnace was already cold?"
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A massive reinforced steel blast door half-open in a dark underground mountain tunnel, scorch marks and glowing molten glass dripping down the threshold forming letters, heavy mist spilling out across shattered bongo drums left on the railway tracks.

